<Window x:Class="WarsOfLibertyLauncher.ProfileWindow"
        xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
        xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
        xmlns:shell="clr-namespace:System.Windows.Shell;assembly=PresentationFramework"
        xmlns:controls="clr-namespace:WarsOfLibertyLauncher.Controls"
        Title="Profile"
        Height="720" Width="1040"
        MinHeight="480" MinWidth="780"
        WindowStartupLocation="CenterScreen"
        WindowStyle="None"
        ResizeMode="CanResize"
        AllowsTransparency="False"
        Background="{DynamicResource MpAppBg}"
        ShowInTaskbar="True">

    <!--
        The player's own profile — standing, record, civilizations, decks and the
        match history — as a window instead of a subtab of MULTIPLAYER.

        WHY IT LEFT THE TAB. That subtab strip and the room-tool cluster beside it
        share one `*` + `Auto` row and NEITHER carries TextTrimming, so the loser is
        not ellipsised — it is painted over. The bar had already been clawed back
        from 1226 px of demand to 1056 against 1078 by shaving padding and the search
        box. Profile is the page on it that is about the VIEWER alone and that nobody
        browses on their way to a room, so it is the one that can leave; the account
        block in the launcher's nav bar was already half-opening it (a two-item menu
        whose first entry jumped to the subtab) and is the conventional door.

        Contract, identical to LobbyWindow — read that file's header for the long
        version, and the non-modal bullet in CLAUDE.md under Runtime conventions:

          * Opened with .Show() from MultiplayerTab, which owns it in a
            single-instance field and drives the body through RenderProfileTab.
          * NO Owner. A WindowStyle=None window that is ALSO ShowInTaskbar=True
            MINIMIZES ITS OWNER when it closes; that is the bug that made closing
            Settings minimize the launcher, and it is why those windows are
            ownerless too. The cost is that it alt-tabs on its own and the launcher
            can sit over it, which is the accepted trade.
          * NO WindowChrome here. App.OnAnyWindowLoaded applies it to every
            WindowStyle=None window, along with the rounded corners and the
            maximize-that-respects-the-taskbar bound. Declaring one would duplicate it.
          * min + max + close, because this is a resizable non-modal window — the
            button policy in CLAUDE.md — and ShowInTaskbar=True so minimize lands on
            a real taskbar button instead of the unstylable desktop stub.

        WIDTH. 1040 is not a round number: the deck grid and its detail panel were
        designed against the ~1014 px ProfileView had inside the tab, and this
        reproduces that once the border and the body margin come off.

        NO UiScale, deliberately, and that follows the newer rule rather than
        breaking the older one. The Workshop and both settings windows came OFF that
        transform because they simply REFLOW, and so does this page: the middle row
        is a star card beside a fixed 295, the stats row is three star cells, the
        decks are a WrapPanel. Nothing here is a bar that cannot give ground, which
        is the only thing that still justifies the transform on the multiplayer tab.
        Keeping it off keeps ClearType at every width; MinWidth covers the rest.
    -->

    <!-- 1 px frame so the dark window reads as a distinct panel instead of melting
         into the (equally dark) launcher behind it. Same reasoning as LobbyWindow:
         AllowsTransparency=False rules out a drop shadow, so a hard edge is the tool
         available. -->
    <Border BorderBrush="{DynamicResource MpRimMedium}"
            BorderThickness="1"
            Background="{DynamicResource MpAppBg}">
        <Grid>
            <Grid.RowDefinitions>
                <RowDefinition Height="Auto"/>   <!-- Title bar (chrome) -->
                <RowDefinition Height="*"/>      <!-- The profile page -->
            </Grid.RowDefinitions>

            <controls:TitleBar Grid.Row="0"
                               x:Name="TitleBarControl"
                               Background="{DynamicResource MpTitleBar}"
                               ShowMinimize="True"
                               ShowMaximize="True"
                               ShowClose="True"/>
            <!-- No sign-out here. It briefly lived in this bar and went back to the
                 account menu in the nav bar, which is where the click that opens this
                 window comes from. The reason it may not go in the PAGE instead still
                 stands, inverted: RenderProfileTab returns early when the active section
                 is Mazos, so BuildProfileHeader is not built there and a link beside the
                 name would vanish the moment you pressed that pill. -->

            <!-- One page-level scroller and no MaxWidth — both carried over from
                 ProfileView unchanged. The 900 px bound the handoff asked for was
                 rejected three times, and the filter chips at the top of the history
                 scroll away with the page: don't "fix" that by nesting a second
                 scroller inside this one. -->
            <Grid Grid.Row="1" x:Name="ProfileContentRoot">
                <ScrollViewer VerticalScrollBarVisibility="Auto"
                              HorizontalScrollBarVisibility="Disabled">
                    <StackPanel x:Name="ProfileBody"
                                Margin="14,16,14,18"
                                HorizontalAlignment="Stretch"/>
                </ScrollViewer>
            </Grid>
        </Grid>
    </Border>
</Window>
